Document a complete inventory of all external-facing services that provide access to non-public organisational or customer data to users over the internet. Include services hosted on the internal network, in your cloud data centre, or on third‑party IaaS/PaaS/SaaS platforms.

 For each listed service, provide:
 - the service name and purpose (for example: VPN server, mail server, customer-facing application, hosted SaaS/PaaS offering);
 - the hosting location (internal network, specific cloud provider and region, or third‑party IaaS/PaaS);
 - the types and classification of data accessible via the service (identify confidential or sensitive data that must not be public).

 Confirm and describe the authentication controls in place for each service:
 - confirm that users are authenticated before being granted access to organisational or customer data or services;
 - list the authentication methods used (for example: username/password, multi-factor authentication, certificate-based authentication, SSO) and any authentication protocols (for example: OAuth, SAML, RADIUS);
 - provide evidence of authentication configuration (configuration files, screenshots, policy excerpts, or architecture diagrams).

 Describe any exceptions where access is intentionally public and justify why the data is appropriate for public access. Exclude such services from the scope of confidential-data authentication requirements and document the rationale.

 Provide details of any external access mechanisms (for example: VPN, remote desktop gateways, API endpoints) and describe how these are protected to ensure only authenticated users can access confidential data.

 Ensure all supporting evidence and descriptions demonstrate compliance with the Cyber Essentials requirement to authenticate users before allowing access to organisational data or services.
